course: dictionary
lesson: 12638
title: Dispel: making doubts go away
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To dispel something is to make it go away. The thing being dispelled can be a feeling, a fear, a doubt, a false belief or even fog. «The warm sun dispelled the morning fog.» The fog did not just get thinner. It was chased away, and the sky was clear.
explain: Dispel is often used with fears and myths. «Her kind words dispelled my fear of the test.» Her words made the fear go away. A myth is a false belief that people wrongly think is true. «The scientist used clear evidence to dispel the myth.» The evidence proved the false belief wrong.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Ask yourself: what was there before, and what is gone now? If the fog is there at the start and gone at the end, dispelled means made to go away.
fact: Dispel is a verb. Its forms are dispel, dispels, dispelled and dispelling.
fact: «Dispelled» means made to go away, such as a fear, a doubt or a false belief.
fact: Less commonly, dispel can mean to scatter something, like fog or clouds.
fact: You can dispel a myth, dispel fears or dispel rumours.
fact: A good check is to swap in the word «chase away» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The warm sun dispelled the thick fog.» What does dispelled mean here? | Sang loudly | *Chased away | Made thicker | Painted
exercise: «Her kind words dispelled my fear.» What did her words do to the fear? | Made it bigger | *Made it go away | Repeated it again and again | Hid it in a box
exercise: «Ben read the evidence and dispelled the myth that goldfish have a three-second memory.» What did Ben do to the myth? | Spread it to his friends | Wrote it in a story | Believed it even more | *Proved it wrong
test: «The scientist used clear evidence to dispel the myth.» What is a myth here? | A type of evidence | A kind of fog | A proven fact in a science book | *A false belief that people wrongly think is true
test: «Rumours spread fast, but the teacher's calm reply dispelled them.» What did the reply do to the rumours? | Wrote them down | Made them louder | Spread them further | *Made them go away
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to dispel, as in scattering fog? | Repeat | Gather | *Scatter | Invent
